================================================================================
SECTION 4: EXCEL DATA STRUCTURE
================================================================================

4.1 Required Columns
---------------------
  Columns are matched by HEADER NAME (case-insensitive), not by position.
  This ensures correct parsing even if column order varies between files.

  Required header names (case-insensitive match):
    - "Date"      : Event date
    - "Time"      : Event time
    - "Event"     : Machine breakdown description (e.g., Motor failure)
    - "Impact"    : Loss description (e.g., 2 hours downtime, 50 pcs scrap)
    - "By Whom"   : Person responsible / who handled the event

  Error Behavior: If any required header is missing, display a clear error
  message listing which headers were not found. Do not silently fail.

4.2 Date and Time Format Handling
-----------------------------------
  Problem: Excel files from different sources may have inconsistent date
  and time formats within the same file or across files.

  Detection and Normalization Rules:

  DATE FORMAT DETECTION:
    The application must detect and parse the following date formats:
      - ISO       : 2024-01-15, 2024/01/15
      - US        : 01/15/2024, 01-15-2024, 1/15/2024
      - EU        : 15/01/2024, 15-01-2024, 15.01.2024
      - Short year: 01/15/24, 15/01/24
      - Text month: Jan 15 2024 / 15 Jan 2024 / January 15 2024
      - Excel serial numbers (e.g., 45307 = 2024-01-15)

    Ambiguity resolution strategy (e.g., is 01/02/2024 Jan 2 or Feb 1?):
      - Scan ALL date values in the column first
      - If any value has day > 12 (e.g., 15/01/2024), use that to infer
        the format pattern (DD/MM or MM/DD) for the entire column
      - If all values are ambiguous (both parts <= 12), default to MM/DD/YYYY
      - Display a notice to the user: "Date format assumed as MM/DD/YYYY.
        If incorrect, please reformat your Excel dates."

  TIME FORMAT DETECTION:
    The application must detect and parse the following time formats:
      - 24-hour      : 14:30, 08:05, 9:30
      - 12-hour      : 2:30 PM, 2:30PM, 2:30 pm
      - With seconds : 14:30:00, 2:30:00 PM
      - Excel fractional days (e.g., 0.604167 = 14:30)
      - Missing time : If time column is empty for a row, default to 00:00
        and flag the row with a warning icon on the timeline

  INTERNAL FORMAT:
    After detection, all dates and times are normalized internally to:
      - Date: JavaScript Date object (UTC)
      - Display format on timeline: YYYY-MM-DD
      - Display format for time  : HH:MM (24-hour)

  ERROR HANDLING:
    - If a date/time value cannot be parsed by any strategy, mark that row
      as "Parse Error" and:
        (a) Skip the row from the timeline
        (b) Display a summary after import: "X of Y rows skipped due to
            unreadable date/time values" with row numbers listed


================================================================================
SECTION 5: FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

5.1 File Upload Module
-----------------------
  FR-1.1: Accept Excel files (.xlsx, .xls format)
  FR-1.2: Support click-to-upload and drag-and-drop
  FR-1.3: Show file name after successful upload
  FR-1.4: Display error message for invalid file types
  FR-1.5: Parse columns by header name matching (see Section 4.1)
  FR-1.6: Display actionable error if required headers are missing,
          listing the specific missing column names
  FR-1.7: Detect and normalize inconsistent date/time formats
          (see Section 4.2 for full rules)
  FR-1.8: Display date format assumption notice when ambiguity
          is resolved by heuristic
  FR-1.9: Display import summary with skipped-row count if any
          date/time values could not be parsed

5.2 Timeline Graph Generation
------------------------------
  FR-2.1: Sort events chronologically by date and time
  FR-2.2: Display horizontal timeline with time axis
  FR-2.3: Show event information per marker:
            - Date and Time label (displayed ABOVE the timeline axis)
            - Event description  (displayed in card BELOW the axis)
            - Impact             (RED background if negative words detected,
                                  GRAY background otherwise)
            - By Whom            (displayed in card BELOW the axis)
  FR-2.4: Auto-calculate timeline scale based on date range
  FR-2.5: Display event markers on timeline axis

5.3 Horizontal Equal-Spacing Layout
--------------------------------------
  FR-3.1: Distribute ALL event markers equally along the horizontal axis
          regardless of actual time gaps between events. This ensures
          that info boxes do not overlap each other.

          Implementation:
            - Divide the available canvas width (minus left/right padding)
              by the maximum events per line (default 6) to determine uniform grid spacing
            - Place each marker exactly on its left-aligned grid column position
            - The timeline axis still shows actual date/time labels per
              marker, but spacing is VISUAL, not proportional to time

  FR-3.2: When event count exceeds the per-line maximum (default: 6),
          split timeline into multiple horizontal lines

  FR-3.3: Order lines from top to bottom chronologically

  FR-3.4: Maintain chronological order across all lines

  FR-3.5: Each line renders within the defined canvas width

  FR-3.6: Event card vertical layout per marker (top to bottom):
            Row 1 - Date and Time label (ABOVE the axis line)
            Row 2 - Timeline axis with marker dot (on the line)
            Row 3 - Short connector line from marker dot downward
            Row 4 - Event info card (BELOW the marker, directly under it)

          Rules:
            - ALL info cards are positioned BELOW the timeline axis
            - No alternating above/below placement
            - Equal horizontal spacing prevents card overlap
            - Each card is horizontally centered on its marker

  FR-3.7: Each event info card contains (top to bottom inside the card):
            - Event description text
            - Impact field (colored background per FR-4.4 / FR-4.5)
            - By Whom text

  Note: Maximum events per line (default: 6) should be a configurable
  constant in the JavaScript source for easy adjustment.

5.4 Negative-Word Detection for Impact Highlighting
------------------------------------------------------
  FR-4.1: Scan the "Impact" field text for negative keywords

  FR-4.2: Negative keyword list (case-insensitive matching):
            scrap, loss, block, broken, reject,
            rejected, blocked, damage, damaged,
            fail, failed, failure, stop, stopped,
            waste, delay, delayed, downtime, breakdown

          Note: This list should be a configurable array constant in
          the JavaScript source for easy extension.

  FR-4.3: Matching rule: substring match within the Impact text
          (e.g., "rejected" matches "reject"; "motor failure" matches
          "fail" and "failure")

  FR-4.4: If ANY negative keyword is found in Impact:
            - Event marker color : RED (#DC3545)
            - Impact text box    : RED background (#DC3545), WHITE text

  FR-4.5: If NO negative keyword is found in Impact:
            - Event marker color : BLUE (#0066CC) - default
            - Impact text box    : GRAY background (#F0F0F0), BLACK text

5.5 Visual Design Requirements
---------------------------------
  FR-5.1: White background for entire application (#FFFFFF)
  FR-5.2: Impact highlighting is conditional - see FR-4.4 and FR-4.5
  FR-5.3: Use Arial font, minimum 12px for all text
  FR-5.4: Event markers clearly visible on timeline axis
  FR-5.5: Ensure sufficient contrast for text on colored backgrounds
          (white text on red backgrounds)

5.6 Responsive Behavior
-------------------------
  FR-6.1: Canvas width auto-fits to window width on initial load
  FR-6.2: On window resize, re-render canvas with debounce delay of 300ms
          to prevent performance degradation during resize drag
  FR-6.3: Maintain minimum readable font size at all canvas widths
  FR-6.4: Keep consistent padding and spacing between event cards

5.7 Export Function
---------------------
  FR-7.1: Provide "Export to PNG" button in the toolbar
  FR-7.2: Capture the entire timeline canvas (all lines)
  FR-7.3: Export with white background (#FFFFFF)
  FR-7.4: Automatically trigger file download to local PC
  FR-7.5: Use filename format: timeline_YYYY-MM-DD_HHMMSS.png
  FR-7.6: Use native canvas.toBlob() for PNG generation

5.8 Demo Data Mode
--------------------
  FR-8.1: Provide a "Load Demo Data" button in the toolbar
  FR-8.2: Button loads an embedded JSON dataset of 8-10 sample events
          using the 5-column structure: Date, Time, Event, Impact, By Whom
  FR-8.3: Demo data must include a MIX of:
            - Events with negative keywords in Impact (to show red markers)
            - Events without negative keywords (to show blue markers)
          Suggested split: ~5 red, ~5 blue
  FR-8.4: Demo data renders the full timeline immediately without file upload
  FR-8.5: Demo data uses realistic machine breakdown event content
  FR-8.6: Demo data should include intentionally varied date/time formats
          to demonstrate the auto-detection capability (e.g., mix of
          "2024-01-15", "01/20/2024", "Feb 3 2024", "15:30", "3:45 PM")


